    {
      "type": "tldr",
      "visible": true,
      "title": "TL;DR",
      "content": {
        "text": "**GAM** repurposes a pretrained Geometric Foundation Model as a shared substrate for action decoding and world modeling, achieving **6.9 ms inference speed** and substantial robustness against camera perturbations."
      },
      "id": "bd0c95a5"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "visible": true,
      "sectionLabel": "",
      "title": "What is a Geometric Foundation Model?",
      "content": {
        "text": "A Geometric Foundation Model (GFM), such as DA3 or VGGT, is a feed-forward transformer designed to map one or more RGB images to dense 3D geometry, outputting per-pixel depth or 3D point maps. Unlike standard vision-language backbones that discard explicit spatial configurations, a GFM preserves dense structural representations across multiple intermediate hidden states, offering a rich perceptual prior for contact-rich robot manipulation."
      },
      "id": "18a3d985",
      "paddingTop": 30,
      "paddingBottom": 10
    },
    {
      "type": "two-column",
      "visible": true,
      "sectionLabel": "",
      "title": "Repurposing GFM Beyond Static Feature Extraction",
      "content": {
        "twoColumnSplit": 50,
        "twoColumnGap": 24,
        "left": "### Standard Paradigms\n\n* **Vision-Language-Action (VLA)** models ground natural language instructions in 2D visual representations but struggle with 3D viewpoint variations.\n* **Video World-Action Models (WAM)** employ large video diffusion substrates to capture temporal world dynamics, yet their latent predictions remain bound to 2D pixel space.\n\n### The GAM Paradigm\nInstead of treating the GFM as a frozen, passive feature extractor via alignment or distillation, **Geometric Action Model (GAM)** explicitly overrides the GFM's deep layers to serve as the policy's own temporal prediction and action decoding engine.",
        "rightMedia": {
          "type": "image",
          "src": "",
          "caption": "Figure 1: Conceptual architectural paradigms of Video WAMs, Geometry-aware VLAs, and GAM.",
          "alt": "Figure 1 from paper — architectural comparison illustrating Video WAMs, Geometry-aware VLAs, and our unified GAM approach."
        }
      },
      "id": "519f09ac",
      "paddingTop": 20,
      "paddingBottom": 10
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "visible": true,
      "sectionLabel": "METHOD",
      "title": "Causal Feature Prediction",
      "content": {
        "text": "GAM splits the GFM backbone at an intermediate layer $L_s$. The shallow layers function as an independent observation encoder. At the split layer, a causal transformer layer forecasts future latent tokens conditioned on language instructions, proprioception, and previous actions. Crucially, the single action token is propagated through the remaining deep GFM blocks alongside predicted future geometric features, enabling joint generation of downstream depth maps and final robot action commands.\n\nThe system optimizes a joint multi-task objective:\n\n$$\\mathcal{L}_{total} = \\lambda_{act}\\mathcal{L}_{act} + \\lambda_{feat}\\mathcal{L}_{feat} + \\lambda_{depth}\\mathcal{L}_{depth}$$"
      },
      "id": "922a1291"
    },
